> Dear Elias,
> Thanks for your prompt response. I have created a simple code as suggested but not sure whether some adjustments could be necessary.  Is it possible to include non-stationerity in the process?  In some pixels (experiencing two seasons in a year) especially in Africa, it is possible to find bi-cyclic pattern. How can we take this situation into account?

It sounds like you need a spatial model?
Either matern2d or the spde models can be used as spatial weight coefficients for you cos&sin temporal functions. If you use the spde models, you can do for example

inla.spde.make.A(mesh, loc, index=index, weights=w*t)
if loc is a 2-column matrix of pixel coordinates, and index is an index vector, for each pixel data indexing into the corresponding coordinate row of loc, and t is a vector of corresponding time points for the observations.

Adding the higher order harmonics is then just a matter of adding more f() terms to the formula.

Finn

